WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.132
- You know when you look at
something that looks hard,

00:00:03.132 --> 00:00:04.427
that was probably easy.

00:00:04.427 --> 00:00:08.428
And I had this firm belief,
which I still believe,

00:00:08.428 --> 00:00:09.877
that people are basically good.

00:00:09.877 --> 00:00:13.719
Don't take no for an answer,
when people out there are

00:00:13.719 --> 00:00:15.445
giving you advice and saying,
"No this will never work."

00:00:15.445 --> 00:00:18.909
If you're passionate about
something and you work hard,

00:00:18.909 --> 00:00:20.394
then I think you'll be successful.

00:00:20.394 --> 00:00:24.561
Code does change the world and
it can change people's lives.

00:00:25.474 --> 00:00:27.650
If somebody does something nice
for you, wouldn't it be nice

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:30.066
in this world if we could
actually start recognizing that.

00:00:30.066 --> 00:00:31.965
I think we've lost a
lot of that in America,

00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:34.093
it's something that here in Europe

00:00:34.093 --> 00:00:35.669
we're a lot more familiar with.

00:00:35.669 --> 00:00:38.195
There are no entrepreneurs
out there that are more

00:00:38.195 --> 00:00:40.926
hard working and innovative
than the small business.

00:00:40.926 --> 00:00:42.926
I didn't sit down and say,

00:00:42.926 --> 00:00:44.612
"Hey I'm going to be innovative today."

00:00:44.612 --> 00:00:47.064
And if I say that you should treat people

00:00:47.064 --> 00:00:48.654
with the benefit of
the doubt, it's because

00:00:48.654 --> 00:00:51.134
I believe in that as a way of life.

00:00:51.134 --> 00:00:53.496
- He's a French born American entrepreneur

00:00:53.496 --> 00:00:54.784
and philanthropist.

00:00:54.784 --> 00:00:56.637
He's the founder of eBay,
where he also served

00:00:56.637 --> 00:00:59.137
as Chairman from 1998 to 2015.

00:01:00.132 --> 00:01:03.708
He has an estimated net
worth of 7.2 billion dollars.

00:01:03.708 --> 00:01:05.868
He's Pierre Omidyar, and
here's my take on his

00:01:05.868 --> 00:01:08.035
top ten rules for success.

00:01:10.864 --> 00:01:13.425
- When you look at the
accomplishments of accomplished

00:01:13.425 --> 00:01:17.592
people and you say, "Boy that
must have been really hard,"

00:01:18.584 --> 00:01:20.690
when you look at
something that looks hard,

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:24.876
that was probably easy, and
conversely, when you look

00:01:24.876 --> 00:01:27.707
at something that looks
easy, that was probably hard.

00:01:27.707 --> 00:01:29.596
And so you're never going to
know which is which until you

00:01:29.596 --> 00:01:31.777
actually go and do it,
so just do and do it,

00:01:31.777 --> 00:01:35.156
try, learn from it, you'll
fail at some things,

00:01:35.156 --> 00:01:37.044
but that's a learning
experience that you need

00:01:37.044 --> 00:01:41.397
so that you can take that
on to the next experience.

00:01:41.397 --> 00:01:45.564
And don't let people who
you may respect and who you

00:01:46.621 --> 00:01:48.510
believe know what they're
talking about, don't let them

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:51.830
tell you it can't be done
because often they will

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:55.521
tell you it can't be done
and it's just cause they

00:01:55.521 --> 00:01:58.070
don't have the courage to try it.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:01.775
I started this just with
the idea of, let's bring the

00:02:01.775 --> 00:02:04.622
power of interactive technology,

00:02:04.622 --> 00:02:06.955
the web, to ordinary people.

00:02:08.033 --> 00:02:12.200
And I had this firm belief,
which I still believe,

00:02:13.254 --> 00:02:14.878
that people are basically good.

00:02:14.878 --> 00:02:19.397
And as I said, if you treat
them well and you treat them

00:02:19.397 --> 00:02:20.973
with the benefit of the doubt,

00:02:20.973 --> 00:02:23.380
you're not going to be disappointed.

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:26.043
And in the modern world,
we're bombarded with

00:02:26.043 --> 00:02:28.262
negative images all the
time about how bad the

00:02:28.262 --> 00:02:30.018
world is, how dangerous the world is,

00:02:30.018 --> 00:02:32.416
and it is true there are some
dangerous things going on

00:02:32.416 --> 00:02:34.396
and there are some bad actors out there

00:02:34.396 --> 00:02:36.756
but the majority of
people out there are just

00:02:36.756 --> 00:02:39.089
good, decent, honest people.

00:02:41.034 --> 00:02:44.127
There's something about
an entrepreneur that is

00:02:44.127 --> 00:02:48.294
somewhat sort of anti-establishment,
somewhat disrespectful

00:02:49.169 --> 00:02:53.033
of the previous generation
and although that can

00:02:53.033 --> 00:02:55.579
grate people the wrong way a little bit,

00:02:55.579 --> 00:02:58.289
that a really important element actually.

00:02:58.289 --> 00:03:01.345
You need to be passionate
about what you want to work on,

00:03:01.345 --> 00:03:06.164
and don't take no for an
answer when people out there

00:03:06.164 --> 00:03:07.382
are giving you advice and saying,

00:03:07.382 --> 00:03:08.544
"No, this will never work."

00:03:08.544 --> 00:03:12.377
I mean you look at eBay,
the idea that in 1995

00:03:13.609 --> 00:03:16.738
that you could create
something over the internet

00:03:16.738 --> 00:03:20.285
which is this brand new
thing that nobody was really

00:03:20.285 --> 00:03:22.521
using at the time, except
for scientists and academics,

00:03:22.521 --> 00:03:26.241
and it would be a place where
strangers could actually

00:03:26.241 --> 00:03:28.525
do business with one
another, could actually

00:03:28.525 --> 00:03:33.175
exchange merchandise for
money without ever meeting,

00:03:33.175 --> 00:03:36.338
and the notion that people
can actually buy and sell cars

00:03:36.338 --> 00:03:38.087
over the internet, that's crazy right?

00:03:38.087 --> 00:03:39.179
Yeah, it's totally crazy.

00:03:39.179 --> 00:03:40.763
In fact, lots and lots of people

00:03:40.763 --> 00:03:42.527
told me that was crazy.

00:03:42.527 --> 00:03:44.205
And I didn't...

00:03:44.205 --> 00:03:45.958
I have to say, I didn't have this vision,

00:03:45.958 --> 00:03:47.141
"Oh it's going to take over
the world, it's going to

00:03:47.141 --> 00:03:49.029
be this fantastic thing overnight."

00:03:49.029 --> 00:03:52.642
I didn't have that kind
of delusion either,

00:03:52.642 --> 00:03:54.294
but I did say, "You know what, there's

00:03:54.294 --> 00:03:57.092
"something worth trying
here, it's not crazy."

00:03:57.092 --> 00:04:00.172
I say that you should pursue your passion,

00:04:00.172 --> 00:04:03.519
if you're passionate about
something and you work hard,

00:04:03.519 --> 00:04:06.092
then I think you'll be successful.

00:04:06.092 --> 00:04:08.749
If you start a business because you think

00:04:08.749 --> 00:04:11.135
you're going to make a lot of money at it,

00:04:11.135 --> 00:04:13.021
then you probably won't be successful,

00:04:13.021 --> 00:04:15.216
because that's the wrong
reason to start a business.

00:04:15.216 --> 00:04:17.681
You have to really believe
in what you're doing,

00:04:17.681 --> 00:04:21.105
be passionate enough
about it so that you will

00:04:21.105 --> 00:04:23.554
put in the hours and the
hard work that it takes

00:04:23.554 --> 00:04:26.874
to actually succeed there,
then you'll be successful.

00:04:26.874 --> 00:04:29.894
- I know I've heard the story
that the first line of code

00:04:29.894 --> 00:04:31.659
that you wrote was in an
apartment in Campbell,

00:04:31.659 --> 00:04:32.692
not too far from here.

00:04:32.692 --> 00:04:34.728
- That's right, just down
the street, that's right.

00:04:34.728 --> 00:04:36.477
- I mean, did you have
any idea, you wrote this,

00:04:36.477 --> 00:04:37.508
you put it out on the internet...

00:04:37.508 --> 00:04:38.341
- Yeah.

00:04:38.341 --> 00:04:39.694
- You didn't sort of do it thinking,

00:04:39.694 --> 00:04:41.667
"Hey, I'm going to build an
83 billion dollar business."

00:04:41.667 --> 00:04:43.916
- No, of course not at all, not at all.

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:46.833
Really it was just this, as I said,

00:04:47.788 --> 00:04:48.862
as a software engineer,

00:04:48.862 --> 00:04:50.936
I wanted to write some
tools that I thought

00:04:50.936 --> 00:04:55.103
could be useful to ordinary
people, regular people.

00:04:56.143 --> 00:04:56.976
I had a day job,

00:04:56.976 --> 00:05:00.643
it started as a nights
and weekends project,

00:05:02.034 --> 00:05:03.927
and it's amazing,

00:05:03.927 --> 00:05:07.227
of course now we all understand,

00:05:07.227 --> 00:05:10.117
code does change the world

00:05:10.117 --> 00:05:14.283
and it can change people's
lives, but I think what makes

00:05:14.283 --> 00:05:18.096
eBay unique, despite the
fact that there are many...

00:05:18.096 --> 00:05:20.314
Sort of now there are more
marketplace businesses

00:05:20.314 --> 00:05:24.481
out there, we are really,
have always been about people,

00:05:25.409 --> 00:05:30.239
it's always been about bringing
tools to ordinary people,

00:05:30.239 --> 00:05:34.157
and giving them the tools
and the opportunities that

00:05:34.157 --> 00:05:36.651
they need, that they can
use to then be successful,

00:05:36.651 --> 00:05:39.309
and define success in their own way.

00:05:39.309 --> 00:05:43.392
So yeah, those early weeks
and months were crazy.

00:05:45.176 --> 00:05:49.219
So I remember one of the
very first items listed,

00:05:49.219 --> 00:05:52.469
this was in 1995, in September of 1995,

00:05:53.898 --> 00:05:56.074
one of the very first items listed was a

00:05:56.074 --> 00:05:58.574
1952 Silver Dawn Royals Royce,

00:06:01.867 --> 00:06:05.350
and the seller, and I wish
we could find the seller,

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:09.176
the seller was amazing
because he or she had done

00:06:09.176 --> 00:06:12.189
this beautiful listing,
and so you could put html

00:06:12.189 --> 00:06:15.618
in there, the seller had
created a server somewhere

00:06:15.618 --> 00:06:18.383
to actually host photos,
back in those days you had to

00:06:18.383 --> 00:06:20.759
actually run your own
server to host photos,

00:06:20.759 --> 00:06:23.256
and actually had this
beautiful listing on there.

00:06:23.256 --> 00:06:25.137
I looked at that and I
said, "This is incredible,

00:06:25.137 --> 00:06:28.702
there is no way anyone will
ever buy a car on the internet"

00:06:28.702 --> 00:06:31.196
Like that's just ridiculous,
that'll never happen.

00:06:31.196 --> 00:06:32.231
(audience laughter)

00:06:32.231 --> 00:06:34.672
Of course now, there's a
car sold every four minutes,

00:06:34.672 --> 00:06:36.910
on a mobile device, it's incredible,

00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:38.064
it's totally incredible.

00:06:38.064 --> 00:06:40.564
So who could've imagined that.

00:06:41.693 --> 00:06:45.068
It was actually in February of '96,

00:06:45.068 --> 00:06:49.235
I came up with the idea of
creating the feedback forum.

00:06:50.251 --> 00:06:53.418
And basically what that
was, was to encourage people

00:06:53.418 --> 00:06:55.332
to give each other feedback based on

00:06:55.332 --> 00:06:57.585
how did their transaction go.

00:06:57.585 --> 00:07:00.521
And since I was getting
all these complaints,

00:07:00.521 --> 00:07:03.423
I assumed that it would
be mostly negative,

00:07:03.423 --> 00:07:06.980
that it was a forum to give
each other negative feedback.

00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:11.842
And so I wrote this little
letter, to the community,

00:07:11.842 --> 00:07:14.353
in February of '96 announcing
the feedback forum,

00:07:14.353 --> 00:07:16.994
and I basically in it I
said, "Hey listen, people are

00:07:16.994 --> 00:07:19.867
"basically good, we all
try to good a good job,

00:07:19.867 --> 00:07:22.309
"we all try to do our
best, but sometimes things

00:07:22.309 --> 00:07:25.056
"don't work out, it would be
great, let's give each other

00:07:25.056 --> 00:07:28.973
"the benefit of the doubt,
but if you're having real

00:07:28.973 --> 00:07:31.807
"problems with somebody, now
there's a feedback forum."

00:07:31.807 --> 00:07:34.336
Basically you don't
have to complain to me,

00:07:34.336 --> 00:07:36.147
but there's a feedback forum.

00:07:36.147 --> 00:07:38.851
"If your complaint is worthy of complaint,

00:07:38.851 --> 00:07:41.096
"then you should do it
publicly and complain in a

00:07:41.096 --> 00:07:43.996
"public forum, but while
you're at it also think about

00:07:43.996 --> 00:07:45.947
"giving praise where praise
is due. If somebody does

00:07:45.947 --> 00:07:48.183
"something nice for you,
wouldn't it be nice in this

00:07:48.183 --> 00:07:50.565
"world if we could actually
start recognizing that,

00:07:50.565 --> 00:07:54.592
"and let's give each other
praise where praise is due."

00:07:54.592 --> 00:07:57.722
And we found that when you
give somebody the opportunity

00:07:57.722 --> 00:07:59.727
to give praise, to give
positive feedback when

00:07:59.727 --> 00:08:02.791
it's well deserved,
people love to do that,

00:08:02.791 --> 00:08:05.034
it's very gratifying
yourself to be able to give

00:08:05.034 --> 00:08:07.911
praise too, to somebody else.

00:08:07.911 --> 00:08:11.115
So anyways, the feedback
forum was just a remarkable,

00:08:11.115 --> 00:08:14.445
incredibly gratifying
testament to human nature

00:08:14.445 --> 00:08:16.184
and the fundamental
goodness of human nature.

00:08:16.184 --> 00:08:17.870
But then also, from a
business perspective,

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:20.834
it was really the thing that
allowed eBay to succeed,

00:08:20.834 --> 00:08:23.689
because it gave people a
chance, a way to know that

00:08:23.689 --> 00:08:26.248
they could actually trust
a complete stranger.

00:08:26.248 --> 00:08:29.212
What I realized through the
eBay experience was that

00:08:29.212 --> 00:08:31.672
people are hungry for
personal communication

00:08:31.672 --> 00:08:36.013
and personal relationships
around a shared area of interest.

00:08:36.013 --> 00:08:38.477
And I received hundreds of
letters, thousands of letter,

00:08:38.477 --> 00:08:40.861
from eBay users that
say, "You know, eBay has

00:08:40.861 --> 00:08:42.208
restored my faith in humanity,

00:08:42.208 --> 00:08:44.461
I've met my best friends on eBay."

00:08:44.461 --> 00:08:45.429
There are people that have been married

00:08:45.429 --> 00:08:47.245
that have met on eBay.

00:08:47.245 --> 00:08:50.798
It's a little bit strange,
it's definitely not the

00:08:50.798 --> 00:08:53.235
majority of the users, but
there's something special

00:08:53.235 --> 00:08:57.695
going on there, and I think
what it is, is when you give

00:08:57.695 --> 00:09:02.100
people a forum in which to
communicate and communicate

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:05.226
around a shared area of
interest, no matter what

00:09:05.226 --> 00:09:07.742
socioeconomic classes they
come from, what neighborhoods

00:09:07.742 --> 00:09:09.523
they come from, or
geographies, they realize that

00:09:09.523 --> 00:09:11.374
they're human beings and
they realize that they

00:09:11.374 --> 00:09:13.040
can be a community today.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:15.247
So what we want to do with
our philanthropy is to just

00:09:15.247 --> 00:09:19.414
help people rediscover
that, especially in America,

00:09:20.823 --> 00:09:22.918
I think we've lost a
lot of that in America.

00:09:22.918 --> 00:09:25.488
It's something that here
in Europe, we're a lot more

00:09:25.488 --> 00:09:28.329
familiar with, we're a lot
more comfortable with a notion

00:09:28.329 --> 00:09:31.868
of an individual being a
part of a community and

00:09:31.868 --> 00:09:35.301
having a responsibility to
be a part of that community.

00:09:35.301 --> 00:09:39.324
In America, through a variety
of reasons that I won't

00:09:39.324 --> 00:09:44.082
go into, I have some thoughts
on it, people have become

00:09:44.082 --> 00:09:45.851
isolated, people are
afraid to talk to their

00:09:45.851 --> 00:09:49.937
neighbors sometimes, but
at the core people do want

00:09:49.937 --> 00:09:53.254
to be part of a community
and all we have to do now

00:09:53.254 --> 00:09:57.092
is figure out how to
rekindle that a little bit

00:09:57.092 --> 00:09:59.009
and that's our mission.

00:09:59.925 --> 00:10:03.679
I'm young, I'm 33 years old,
my wife and I are 33 years old,

00:10:03.679 --> 00:10:05.996
we've got at least 50 good years

00:10:05.996 --> 00:10:08.306
ahead of us to work on this,

00:10:08.306 --> 00:10:10.723
and it's going to be a lifetime pursuit.

00:10:10.723 --> 00:10:12.809
There are no entrepreneurs
out there that are more

00:10:12.809 --> 00:10:16.046
hardworking and innovative
than the small business owner.

00:10:16.046 --> 00:10:18.066
I mean like give me a break.

00:10:18.066 --> 00:10:19.205
(audience applause)

00:10:19.205 --> 00:10:22.123
So Silicon Valley, look at
these wonderful entrepreneurs,

00:10:22.123 --> 00:10:25.968
and of course, we all work
really hard in Silicon Valley,

00:10:25.968 --> 00:10:30.135
here as well, but it is really
hard to run a small business,

00:10:32.588 --> 00:10:35.673
even though eBay's growth was very fast.

00:10:35.673 --> 00:10:37.512
In those early days I learned some of

00:10:37.512 --> 00:10:40.048
the difficulties myself, as an individual,

00:10:40.048 --> 00:10:42.881
just having to deal with all that.

00:10:44.246 --> 00:10:47.714
That, for me, is true entrepreneurship.

00:10:47.714 --> 00:10:49.925
Now what people here in
Silicon Valley also do,

00:10:49.925 --> 00:10:53.425
is we have this incredible sense of vision

00:10:56.132 --> 00:10:59.747
of what's possible in the
world, and I think to be

00:10:59.747 --> 00:11:01.772
a successful entrepreneur
you have to have also

00:11:01.772 --> 00:11:05.005
a little bit of self delusion in your own

00:11:05.005 --> 00:11:07.228
ability to make that happen.

00:11:07.228 --> 00:11:09.420
I didn't sit down and say,

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:13.184
"Hey, I'm going to be innovative today."

00:11:13.184 --> 00:11:16.895
It was really trying to
experiment with new tools.

00:11:16.895 --> 00:11:20.672
I'm a software engineer so
I'm basically the latest,

00:11:20.672 --> 00:11:23.812
coolest, shiny toy at
the time was the web,

00:11:23.812 --> 00:11:27.118
was interactivity, and the way I expressed

00:11:27.118 --> 00:11:30.457
my creativity was through writing code.

00:11:30.457 --> 00:11:34.263
Fast forward to Labor Day weekend of 1995,

00:11:34.263 --> 00:11:37.333
I sat down at my computer and
I started writing a script,

00:11:37.333 --> 00:11:41.500
a purled script, to create a
very basic auction mechanism.

00:11:43.912 --> 00:11:47.371
The code was really simple,
it allowed you to list an item

00:11:47.371 --> 00:11:50.123
for sale, have a title
and a description, and a

00:11:50.123 --> 00:11:53.696
starting price, and you
could also see a list of all

00:11:53.696 --> 00:11:55.466
the items that were listed,
obviously that was important,

00:11:55.466 --> 00:11:57.351
and then you could bid on an item.

00:11:57.351 --> 00:11:59.009
If you think about the retail environment,

00:11:59.009 --> 00:12:01.222
where people are buying things
in a retail environment,

00:12:01.222 --> 00:12:02.578
the retailer has a whole bunch of control.

00:12:02.578 --> 00:12:06.049
They choose the products,
they design the store

00:12:06.049 --> 00:12:08.341
or the catalog, if they
train the salespeople,

00:12:08.341 --> 00:12:10.863
they control the experience,
and if there's a problem

00:12:10.863 --> 00:12:14.680
with a salesperson they
retrain and so on and so forth.

00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:17.777
At eBay, our customer's
experience is based on how one

00:12:17.777 --> 00:12:21.895
customer interacts with
another customer, and you

00:12:21.895 --> 00:12:24.477
can't control customer
behavior, so the only thing

00:12:24.477 --> 00:12:27.284
you can do is have a certain set of values

00:12:27.284 --> 00:12:31.199
that you encourage people
to adopt, and the only way

00:12:31.199 --> 00:12:33.211
your customers are going
to adopt those values

00:12:33.211 --> 00:12:36.205
is if they see you living
those values as well.

00:12:36.205 --> 00:12:38.445
So when I say that I believe
people are basically good,

00:12:38.445 --> 00:12:40.457
it's because I believe
people are basically good,

00:12:40.457 --> 00:12:43.080
it's not something that
I came up with for eBay.

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:45.343
And if I say that you
should treat people with the

00:12:45.343 --> 00:12:48.205
benefit of the doubt, it's
because I believe in that as

00:12:48.205 --> 00:12:51.780
a way of life and we have
to do it internally at eBay

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:55.754
at the company as well,
because if we don't then

00:12:55.754 --> 00:12:59.372
eventually that seeps through
and customers will see that,

00:12:59.372 --> 00:13:02.314
and that will harm our business
because we can't control

00:13:02.314 --> 00:13:05.690
customer behavior, so our
business is based on that.

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:06.845
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:13:06.845 --> 00:13:10.623
I made this video because
Mortesa Dariani asked me to.

00:13:10.623 --> 00:13:12.374
So if there's a famous
entrepreneur that you want me

00:13:12.374 --> 00:13:14.332
to profile next, leave
it in the comments below

00:13:14.332 --> 00:13:15.454
and I'll see what I can do.

00:13:15.454 --> 00:13:17.544
I'd also love to know which
of Pierre's top ten rules

00:13:17.544 --> 00:13:20.646
hit you the hardest,
meant the most to you,

00:13:20.646 --> 00:13:22.783
leave it in the comments and
I'll join the discussion.

00:13:22.783 --> 00:13:24.314
Thank you so much for watching,

00:13:24.314 --> 00:13:27.897
continue to believe,
and I'll see you soon.

